  "title": "Structural vs. Functional Plasticity",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "Learn Structural vs. Functional Plasticity in one minute.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "When the brain changes, it can change in two very different ways, and both happen simultaneously.",
      "visual": "🔀"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Structural plasticity refers to physical changes: new synapses form, dendrites branch, and sometimes new neurons grow.",
      "visual": "🌿"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Functional plasticity means the brain reassigns tasks from damaged areas to healthy ones, like rerouting traffic.",
      "visual": "🚦"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "After a stroke destroys a motor region, surrounding areas can gradually take over the lost function through functional plasticity.",
      "visual": "🔧"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Both types work together. Structural changes build new roads while functional changes redirect the traffic.",
      "visual": "🏗️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "The brain rebuilds its hardware and reassigns its software at the same time.",
      "visual": "💻"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is functional plasticity?",
    "options": [
      "The brain reassigning tasks from damaged to healthy areas",
      "The physical growth of new synapses",
      "The death of unused neurons"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
